#
# rocm-ernic persistent device naming rules.
#
# Renames the network interface and the InfiniBand device of the emulated
# RDMA NIC to stable names, so guests always see rocm-ernic<n> and
# rocm-rdma-ernic<n> instead of PCI-topology names.
#
# The device is identified by the FULL four-field tuple:
#
#   vendor            0x1dd8   AMD Pensando Systems
#   device            0x1002   DSC Ethernet Controller
#   subsystem_vendor  0x1dd8
#   subsystem_device  0x5400   ROCm Emulated RDMA NIC
#
# All four are required. rocm-ernic@3fb74d1 moved the emulated device from
# 1dd8:100a to 1dd8:1002, and 1dd8:1002 is a REAL Pensando DSC Ethernet
# Controller id that physical cards ship with -- hwdata lists ~23 real
# subsystem variants of it. Only the subsystem pair distinguishes the
# emulation, so a vendor+device match would rename a real Pensando NIC on any
# host that had one. The in-tree ionic driver's own PCI table wildcards the
# subsystem (sv*sd*) and cannot tell them apart; udev is the only layer that
# can, which is why the discrimination lives here.
#
# Matching the old id is what cost 32 fleet guests 18 hours: the rules said
# 0x100a, the device was 0x1002, nothing was renamed, and
# rocm-ernic-netcfg.service died at boot on a NIC name that never appeared.
#
# Installed into guests by ansible/playbooks/roles/ernic_fleet_guest
# (ernic_fleet_udev_rules_src). By hand:
#
#   sudo cp udev/99-rocm-ernic.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#   sudo udevadm trigger

# --- Network interface naming ---
#
# The four ids are plain files under /sys/class/net/<if>/device/, so they are
# reachable with ATTR{device/...} on the netdev itself. Note that
# `udevadm info -a` on a netdev does not print these rows -- only the parent
# walk shows them -- so "absent from udevadm info -a" is not evidence the
# attribute is missing.
#
# The index is this device's POSITION AMONG THE MATCHING PCI DEVICES, not %n
# and not the next free name.
#
# %n is the kernel number -- the trailing digits of the sysname -- and every
# emulated NIC comes up as "...np0", so %n is 0 for all of them and the second
# rename fails with the name already in use. Unreachable while a fleet had one
# NIC per guest; certain at ERNIC_PER_VM > 1.
#
# A free-index search ("first rocm-ernicN that does not exist yet") fixes the
# collision but is ORDER-DEPENDENT: it numbers in udev event order, which is a
# race. Measured on a 32-guest fleet with two NICs each -- guest 3 came up with
# rocm-ernic1 on PCI 01:00.0 and rocm-ernic0 on 02:00.0, while guests 7 and 20
# got the opposite. The addresses were still right, because netcfg derives each
# from that NIC's own MAC, but the NAME could not be relied on to say which
# device it was.
#
# Walking the PCI devices in bus order fixes that. gen-compose passes the
# vfio-user sockets to QEMU in ordinal order, QEMU assigns PCI slots in the
# order it is given them, and the sysfs glob sorts by BDF -- so position here
# tracks the global NIC ordinal, and therefore the MAC and the address that
# netcfg derives from it. Verified across three guests: 01:00.0 is always the
# lower ordinal. rocm-ernic0 is now always the guest's lowest-addressed ernic,
# on every boot.
#
# The four ids are re-checked inside the loop rather than trusted from the
# rule match: the loop walks every PCI device, and a real Pensando card shares
# 1dd8:1002 (see the header). grep -qx rather than $(cat) keeps the whole
# program free of double quotes, which udev does not parse inside PROGRAM="".
#
# $$ throughout: udev reads a bare $ as the start of its own substitution, so
# a shell variable has to be written doubled. %p is the devpath, which for a
# netdev ends in /net/<name> under the PCI device it belongs to.
#
# KERNEL!= makes it idempotent. udev refuses to rename an interface that has
# already been renamed, so without the guard a re-triggered event would
# recompute a name for an already-correct device and log a failed rename --
# harmless, but it reads like the bug this rule exists to prevent.

SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", KERNEL!="rocm-ernic*", \
  ATTR{device/vendor}=="0x1dd8", \
  ATTR{device/device}=="0x1002", \
  ATTR{device/subsystem_vendor}=="0x1dd8", \
  ATTR{device/subsystem_device}=="0x5400", \
  DRIVERS=="ionic", \
  PROGRAM="/bin/sh -c 'p=%p; self=$${p%%/net/*}; self=$${self##*/}; i=0; \
    for d in /sys/bus/pci/devices/*; do \
      grep -qx 0x1dd8 $$d/vendor 2>/dev/null || continue; \
      grep -qx 0x1002 $$d/device 2>/dev/null || continue; \
      grep -qx 0x1dd8 $$d/subsystem_vendor 2>/dev/null || continue; \
      grep -qx 0x5400 $$d/subsystem_device 2>/dev/null || continue; \
      [ $${d##*/} = $$self ] && { echo rocm-ernic$$i; exit 0; }; \
      i=$$((i+1)); \
    done; \
    exit 1'", \
  NAME="%c"

# --- InfiniBand device naming ---
#
# Renames the RDMA device from the PCI-topology name (e.g. rocep2s0) to
# rocm-rdma-ernic<n>.
#
# The IB device's own sysfs directory carries none of the four ids -- it has
# node_guid, hca_type and so on, and `device` is a symlink to the PCI device.
# So this rule matches with ATTRS{} off that PCI parent instead. All four come
# from the one parent, which satisfies udev's one-parent-per-rule restriction.
#
# DRIVERS=="ionic|ionic_rdma" is deliberately loose: the parent reports
# DRIVERS=="ionic" (ionic_rdma is an auxiliary driver, not a PCI one), and the
# alternative costs nothing if that ever changes. The tuple, not the driver
# name, is what makes this rule specific.
#
# The system rule 60-rdma-persistent-naming.rules renames the device from its
# kernel-registered name (ionic_%d) to a PCI-topology name via rdma_rename
# before this rule runs, so %k is stale by the time we see it. The PCI part of
# $devpath is not: the sysfs directory moves with the rename but its parent
# does not, so $pci/infiniband/ always lists the device's current name. That is
# also what makes this rule act only on the device the event is about -- an
# earlier version scanned all of /sys/class/infiniband and renamed every match
# to the same hard-coded rocm-rdma-ernic0, so a guest with two emulated devices
# had the second rename fail.
#
# Reading the current name this way also removes the reason for the old
# "(sleep 2; ...) &": udev runs 60- before 99- within one event, so rdma_rename
# has already happened and there is nothing to wait for. Backgrounding it meant
# the rename completed some seconds after udev settled, which a caller checking
# for the device could lose a race against.
#
# The rocm-rdma-ernic* guard makes this idempotent -- without it a re-triggered
# event would rename an already-correct device to the next free index.
#
# The index comes from the PCI bus-order position, exactly as the netdev rule
# above computes it, and for the same reason -- plus a sharper one.
#
# A free-index search ("first rocm-rdma-ernicN that does not exist yet") is not
# merely order-dependent here, it is a TOCTOU race that LOSES DEVICES. udev
# runs one worker per PCI function concurrently, so on a two-NIC guest both
# workers evaluate the while-loop before either has renamed anything, both
# settle on i=0, and both call `rdma dev set ... rocm-rdma-ernic0`. One wins;
# the other fails and the device keeps its PCI-topology name:
#
#   (udev-worker)[456]: ionic_1: Process '/bin/sh -c ... ' failed with exit code 1.
#
# Measured on the 32-guest dense-fleet (ERNIC_PER_VM=2, 64 devices): 13 guests
# correct, 13 with the IB index CROSSED against the netdev index, and 6 where
# the second rename failed outright and left rocep3s0. 19 of 32 wrong.
#
# The crossed half is the dangerous half. Those guests resolve every name,
# ibv_devinfo is happy, and `ib_send_bw -d rocm-rdma-ernic0` runs fine --
# against the NIC addressed .18 instead of .17. Anything that pairs an IB
# device name with the address of the like-numbered netdev is silently talking
# to the wrong device and getting plausible numbers back.
#
# Deriving the index from the device's own BDF removes the shared mutable
# state, so there is nothing to race on, and makes rocm-rdma-ernic<n> and
# rocm-ernic<n> the same physical device BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by luck.
# Verified by scrambling both IB names on all 32 guests and re-triggering
# concurrently: 64/64 came back correctly named and correctly paired.
#
# The four ids are re-checked inside the loop for the same reason the netdev
# rule re-checks them: the loop walks every PCI device, and 1dd8:1002 is also
# a real Pensando card.

SUBSYSTEM=="infiniband", ACTION=="add", \
  DRIVERS=="ionic|ionic_rdma", \
  ATTRS{vendor}=="0x1dd8", \
  ATTRS{device}=="0x1002", \
  ATTRS{subsystem_vendor}=="0x1dd8", \
  ATTRS{subsystem_device}=="0x5400", \
  RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'pci=/sys$devpath; pci=$${pci%%/infiniband/*}; \
    self=$${pci##*/}; i=0; found=no; \
    for d in /sys/bus/pci/devices/*; do \
      grep -qx 0x1dd8 $$d/vendor 2>/dev/null || continue; \
      grep -qx 0x1002 $$d/device 2>/dev/null || continue; \
      grep -qx 0x1dd8 $$d/subsystem_vendor 2>/dev/null || continue; \
      grep -qx 0x5400 $$d/subsystem_device 2>/dev/null || continue; \
      [ $${d##*/} = $$self ] && { found=yes; break; }; \
      i=$$((i+1)); \
    done; \
    [ $$found = yes ] || exit 1; \
    for ibdev in $$pci/infiniband/*; do \
      [ -d $$ibdev ] || continue; \
      cur=$${ibdev##*/}; \
      case $$cur in rocm-rdma-ernic*) continue;; esac; \
      /usr/bin/rdma dev set $$cur name rocm-rdma-ernic$$i; \
    done'"
